The Cauchy distribution has density
$
  f(x) = 1/(ppi (1 + x^2)) quad quad "for" x in RR,
$
and its MGF is
$
  m(theta) = integral_(-oo)^(oo) ee^(theta x)/(ppi (1 + x^2)) dif x = cases(
    1 & quad "for" theta = 0,
    oo & quad "otherwise".)
$
Hence $X, 2X, 3X, ...$ all have the same MGF while having different distributions. The finiteness of the MGF on an open interval of values of $theta$ is therefore a necessary hypothesis in the uniqueness theorem.
